  "body": "### Summary\nAs a member who receives an MP whose subject happens to be the MPStorage subject, I want it to stay unread until I open it, so that the storage sync running in the background does not hide a new message from me.\n\n### Problem\nThe storage search (`MPStorageTransport.findThread`, `Packages/HFRKit/Sources/HFRClient/MPStorage/MPStorageTransport.swift:66-92` at `dev-swift` 97a060bd) GETs the first page of every storage-subject row it meets before the account's own one, to see whether its first message carries the account's edit link (#1200 P1). Captured on #1203 (PR #1367, HFR-PROTOCOL §6c LOT-038): **that GET alone marks the thread read** for the account (`R1203a` → `R1203b` → `R1203c`: « Nouveau message » → « Pas de nouveau message », `sujetCase8` 0 → 1, the list's unread count down by one). The row is kept visible on purpose (#1200 P1), but a member's new MP with that subject is marked read without being opened. Legacy took the first storage-subject row as the account's thread (HFR-PROTOCOL §6c LOT-038), so it read that row's thread too; whether this is worse or equal to legacy depends on the row order.\n\n### Options (needs a choice before coding)\n- Skip a row that does not name the storage recipient as its only interlocutor before fetching it (the account's own rows do, `R13u1`, `R333b`, `R1203a` row 47); fetch only rows that might be the account's. Check what that does to a storage thread whose recipient row is aliased or renamed.\n- Or keep the GET and accept the side effect, documented.\n\n### Sources\n`R1203a`, `R1203b`, `R1203c`, `W1203` (PR #1367); found by the Fable review of the #1203 part (finding 2).\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] A storage-subject row from another member is not fetched by the storage search (or the choice to keep it is recorded in HFR-PROTOCOL §6c LOT-038).\n- [ ] The search still finds the account's thread on `R1203a` (`skipsForeignThreadWithStorageSubject`).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_\n\n",
